   statement: "A claim a brief or plan uses as a LOAD-BEARING PREMISE of a decision is verified at the source before it is acted on, whether or not the controller tagged it VERIFY-FIRST; incidental facts are not. The tag survives as a pointer - it says where the controller already doubts - but is no longer the gate: nobody tags the premise they are sure of, so a tag-gated duty catches only known unknowns, and the misses cluster in the untagged set by construction. Refuting a premise is a valid completion (keep + record), never a failed task. Complements proc-07 rather than replacing it: proc-07 cuts on SUBJECT (tooling and dependency behavior, wherever it appears), this cuts on ROLE (premise of a decision, whatever its subject); neither subsumes the other."
   steelman: "A brief carries dozens of claims; a duty to re-verify all of them turns every task into an audit and doubles its cost, and an unenforceable rule gets ignored - a narrow rule that holds beats a broad one nobody follows. The VERIFY-FIRST tag exists precisely so the controller can point at what matters, and the controller is usually right about what matters. Rejected because the evidence runs the other way: every recorded miss to date was untagged, which is not bad luck but the mechanism - a controller cannot tag a doubt it does not have. Scoping to load-bearing-premise-of-a-decision, rather than to every claim, keeps the cost bounded without keeping the blind spot."
