 - id: proc-verification-step-must-be-falsifiable
   kind: pattern
   tier: 2
   source: agent-emergent
   nature: process
   domain: process
   statement: "A verification step whose passing expectation is an ABSENCE - 'expected: no output', 'expected: exit 0', 'expected: no hits' - is itself verified by making it produce output once. An absence-expectation passes identically when the check works, when the check is malformed, and when the thing it greps for cannot syntactically exist; the three are indistinguishable from the green result, so an unfalsified absence-check is decoration that reads as coverage. Trigger is readable: you are writing an expected result that is empty. Handle: break the thing deliberately, watch the step go red, restore it. The same move the plan already mandates for its guards, applied to its own verification steps. Extends to a grep used as EVIDENCE in a document, not only to a step in a plan: nobody re-runs a negative, so the method has to be sound the first time - a grep returning nothing looks identical whether it was the right grep or the wrong one, and the reader has no way to tell which they are looking at."
   steelman: "Absence-checks are the cheapest verification there is and most of them are trivially correct; demanding a red-run proof for every grep doubles the step count of a plan to catch a class of defect that costs one wasted grep when it fires. Rejected because the cost is not a wasted grep: a vacuous check is worse than no check, because the plan and its reviewer both then believe the property is guarded. The red-run proof is seconds, and it is exactly the discipline TDD already imposes on tests - a test you never saw fail is not evidence, and neither is a grep you never saw match."
